<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gediminas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1275–<a href="/years/1341">1341</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Gedimin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gediminas was Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from about 1316 until his death and founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminids">Gediminid dynasty</a>. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> his capital, expanded his realm deep into the lands of Rus' weakened by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, and in letters of 1323 invited Western merchants, craftsmen and clergy to settle. Though he stayed pagan, he tolerated Christians, and his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kestutis">Kęstutis</a> continued his expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gediminas.jpg" alt="Gediminas" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alessandro Guagnini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giedzimin._%D0%93%D0%B5%D0%B4%D0%B7%D1%96%D0%BC%D1%96%D0%BD_(A._Guagnini,_1578).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
